00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.822
Catrat is the most common cause of treatable blindness globally.

2
00:00:10.822 --> 00:00:18.917
It is important to be able to examine a patient and confirm the cause of treatable catrat.

3
00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:24.155
Start by asking if the cyclose has been gradual or sudden.

4
00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:31.774
If one or both eyes are affected, and if there has been any pain or readiness.

5
00:00:31.774 --> 00:00:36.060
Next, test visual acuity for distance and in A.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.060
Use a pinhole to make sure the lack of glasses are not the cause.

7
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.060
Then look at the pupils.

8
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.060
Are they black, round and equal?

9
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.060
Do they react to light?

10
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.060
If you have a bright light, check if the pupil constricts.

11
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.060
In advanced cataract, the pupil can look white.

12
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.060
Even in cataract, the pupil should still react to light.

13
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:11.060
Examine the front of the eye more closely with an acolyte or with some device.

14
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:17.060
Check to make sure nothing else is wrong with the eye, especially a cornea scar.

15
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:24.060
With an acolyte, you can also check for the yellow or reindefundal reflex.

16
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.060
In an eye with cataract, there can be dark spots.

17
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.380
Patches missing or be completely absent, looking

18
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.700
dark or even white in mature cataracts.

19
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.872
If the vision is poor and the fanroly flex looks okay, then there may

20
00:01:41.872 --> 00:01:42.980
be other reasons

21
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.020
for loss of vision.

22
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.500
This is especially the case if the pupil does not constrict well to light.

23
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.100
Examination of the back of the eye will help wake out what is wrong.

24
00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:02.220
There may be a problem with the optic nerve or retina or both.

25
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:08.144
The nerve may have a large gap due to glaucoma or be paired from

26
00:02:08.144 --> 00:02:10.260
loss of blood supply, compression

27
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.620
or lack of food.

28
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:20.900
The retina may be damaged by diabetes, scarred by infection or may be detached.

29
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.820
In this case, cardiac surgery may not improve the vision.

30
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:33.100
In summary, when assessing for cataract, take a history, test visiography, look at the

31
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.460
front of the eye to confirm cataract and exclude other diseases.

32
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:45.660
Look to see if the bipus are round, black, symmetrical and reactive to light, and if

33
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.340
